# Base system
base
linux
# The linux-firmware metapackage pulls ~773 MB of blobs, and firmware is already
# compressed, so squashfs cannot claw any of it back - it lands on the ISO
# roughly 1:1. Only the subpackages an x86 machine can actually use are listed;
# the baked model eats about a gigabyte of the release budget, so everything
# without a consumer in this image is dropped: qcom (Snapdragon SoC - phones/ARM
# laptops), mellanox, qlogic, nfp and liquidio (datacenter NICs and Fibre
# Channel HBAs), other (the long tail of obscure devices; the common Wi-Fi
# families below are kept), marvell (legacy laptop Wi-Fi), cirrus (laptop
# smart-amp DSP - a headless box has no speakers), broadcom (mostly pre-2015
# laptop Wi-Fi), and the radeon and nvidia GPU families. radeon is pre-GCN3
# silicon, older than RADV supports; nvidia firmware only pays off with the
# NVIDIA driver stack, which is the CUDA ISO variant (packages-cuda.x86_64).
# linux-firmware-amdgpu stays despite being the largest single item: the
# Vulkan llama.cpp in this image offloads to AMD GPUs, and the amdgpu kernel
# driver cannot bind the hardware without its firmware. Intel iGPU firmware
# rides along in linux-firmware-intel.
linux-firmware-intel
linux-firmware-amdgpu
linux-firmware-atheros
linux-firmware-realtek
linux-firmware-mediatek
intel-ucode
amd-ucode
mkinitcpio
mkinitcpio-archiso
mkinitcpio-nfs-utils
sudo
polkit
dbus
# checkupdates, for the LiteAI tools: package_query lists pending updates
# against a copy of the sync database, so the AI never has to run `pacman -Sy`
# - which would leave the real database half-synced and the machine one command
# away from a partial upgrade.
pacman-contrib

# Live medium / installer tooling
arch-install-scripts
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
gptfdisk
parted
rsync
efibootmgr
grub
syslinux
memtest86+
memtest86+-efi
util-linux

# Networking
networkmanager
wpa_supplicant
wireless-regdb
openssh
wget
curl
ca-certificates

# The local AI stack.
#
# nodejs and npm run pi (the coding agent the machine boots into) and the
# LiteAI MCP server. llama.cpp itself is NOT a package: scripts/fetch-llamacpp.sh
# stages a prebuilt upstream binary into /opt/llama.cpp, which keeps builds
# reproducible and free of AUR dependencies. base-devel, cmake and python exist
# so the machine can rebuild llama.cpp with a different backend (ROCm, SYCL)
# later - the documented upgrade path for anyone whose hardware deserves
# something the shipped binary does not offer.
nodejs
npm
cmake
base-devel

# The Vulkan runtime the staged llama.cpp needs: upstream's x64 build is
# compiled with GGML_VULKAN and links against the loader, whose ICD manifests
# point it at Mesa's RADV (AMD) and ANV (Intel) drivers. With no Vulkan device
# present the binary simply infers on the CPU, so a machine without a usable
# GPU pays nothing but disk. NVIDIA's ICD ships in nvidia-utils, whose display
# stack and kernel module have no business in the standard image - NVIDIA
# cards are what the CUDA ISO variant is for.
vulkan-icd-loader
vulkan-radeon
vulkan-intel

